From 5ab071e228eaf63f1bbc542e0722cf485f202a1a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 19:29:51 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: remove the temporary per-stage frame-cost diagnostic
 (0037)

Removal was planned at landing (patch 0037's own first line), and Round 1
of the 486DX2-50 campaign has answered the question it existed for. This
is that removal, and it is the shipping configuration for Round 2's KPI
binaries; a diagnostic build for Round 2 re-applies 0037's diff locally
on top of the series, with the CMOS RTC witness added, and is never
committed.

What 0037 bought, measured on real hardware (486DX2-50 + Mach64, one
natural life, windows 1-13 of 300 frames, all on the SDL clock):

  render 32.3 ms, blowup 11.9, present 2.4, tail 7.4      = 54 ms of game work
  engine pump 32.4 ms (3.23 SDL_DOSAudioPump calls at 10.0 ms each)
  pacer 10.3 ms (every frame over budget: one SDL_Delay(0) yield, i.e.
  one cooperative audio-thread iterate)                     = 43 ms of audio
  total 98.8 ms/frame; 4.2 chunks of 23 ms audio produced per frame

Audio conversion (the game's double-multiply callback, then SDL's 16-bit
to float, stereo to mono, float to 8-bit passes for the PicoGUS's U8
mono device) is 43% of every frame on this CPU. The low tier does about
40% of that per chunk and measured 14.79 fps in the same round, right
at the budget; the high tier measured about 10 fps, with the engine
clock losing BIOS ticks on every high-tier life (the launch-to-title
wall clock exceeded Game time by 80-166 s where the low tier showed
33-60 s, and both WAVs copy in under 8 s). The sampler split was
2256 hits / 85 misses / 59 transient per frame. The spans summed to
451.9 s over a life the engine timed at 452 s: nothing unaccounted for.

Everything above is recorded in docs/PLAN.md's measured-constants table
and the Round 1 benchmark files, so removing the instrumentation costs
no knowledge. Keeping it would cost a real, if small, per-frame tax, and
docs/BENCHMARK-PLAN.md's INVALID rule bars any diagnostic from a KPI
binary.

Patch 0036 (the SDL-clock percentile capture) is untouched.

